1
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:13,200
This is Bonnie Parker,
the most famous outlaw girl in America,

2
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:17,440
and almost everything you think you know
about her was printed in a newspaper.

3
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:22,898
Together with Clyde Barrow,
she spent two years robbing gas stations

4
00:00:22,918 --> 00:00:28,140
and corner shops, while the papers turned
two broke kids from Dallas into legends.

5
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:34,680
In April 1933, police raided
their hideout in Joplin, Missouri,

6
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:40,240
and found the one thing outlaws should
never leave behind: a camera full of film.

7
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:47,160
On one frame, Bonnie posed with a pistol
and a borrowed cigar, just for fun.

8
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:54,400
Within days that joke was
on front pages across America,

9
00:00:54,420 --> 00:00:57,540
and Bonnie became
the cigar-smoking gun girl.

10
00:01:00,660 --> 00:01:04,250
There was just one problem:
Bonnie didn't smoke cigars,

11
00:01:04,270 --> 00:01:07,660
and she spent the rest of her short
life trying to tell everyone.

12
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:13,700
So how does a joke turn two broke kids
into the most hunted couple in America,

13
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:17,800
and how does one patient man catch
them without ever chasing them?

14
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:25,460
It all starts in West Dallas in the 1920s,
in a slum so poor that people called

15
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:31,020
it the Devil's Back Porch, where Clyde
Barrow grew up with almost nothing.

16
00:01:33,940 --> 00:01:38,600
As a teenager, he got arrested
for a truck full of stolen turkeys.

17
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:43,820
Meanwhile,
a few miles away in Cement City,

18
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:47,260
Bonnie Parker wrote poems
and dreamed of the stage.

19
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:52,520
But at fifteen she married
a boy named Roy Thornton

20
00:01:52,540 --> 00:01:57,520
and had their names tattooed in two
hearts, a very permanent decision.

21
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:04,810
Roy soon landed in prison, so at nineteen
she was a waitress still wearing his ring,

22
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:07,320
waiting for something exciting to happen.

23
00:02:09,440 --> 00:02:12,710
In January 1930 she met
Clyde in a friend's kitchen,

24
00:02:12,730 --> 00:02:16,840
and something exciting finally happened:
a few weeks later he was arrested.

25
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:28,420
Bonnie smuggled
a pistol into the Waco jail.

26
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:37,300
Clyde broke out, and a week later
he was caught again in Ohio.

27
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:52,460
This time he was sent to Eastham,
one of the harshest prison farms in Texas.

28
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:00,080
There another inmate attacked him again
and again,

29
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:04,960
until Clyde killed him with a pipe,
and it was the first life he took.

30
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:10,640
To get out of the fields,
Clyde let an axe fall on his own foot.

31
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:16,280
Six days later: paroled anyway.

32
00:03:17,660 --> 00:03:20,040
He swore he would die before going back,

33
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:24,260
but how do you stay free when every
policeman in Texas knows your face?

34
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:28,690
Bonnie was waiting outside
the gate with the engine running,

35
00:03:28,710 --> 00:03:32,100
and this time they had a plan:
keep moving.

36
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:38,090
Clyde's plan was simple: steal a fast car,

37
00:03:38,110 --> 00:03:42,420
rob small places with little cash
and no guards, and keep driving.

38
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:48,530
Most of their robberies were gas stations
and grocery stores,

39
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:51,220
and many of them paid only a few dollars.

40
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:57,560
Their first big plan was to rob
a hardware store for guns,

41
00:03:57,580 --> 00:04:02,380
but the car got stuck in the mud,
so they tried to escape on stolen mules.

42
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Bonnie was caught
and spent two months in jail,

43
00:04:09,020 --> 00:04:13,920
where she wrote a long poem about
an outlaw girl called Suicide Sal.

44
00:04:15,900 --> 00:04:20,470
While she was locked up, one of Clyde's
robberies went wrong in Hillsboro,

45
00:04:20,490 --> 00:04:23,880
and a shopkeeper named
John Bucher was shot dead.

46
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,320
When the grand jury let Bonnie go,
she promised her mother to stay

47
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:34,020
away from Clyde, and she kept
that promise for a few weeks.

48
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:39,850
That August, at a country dance
in Oklahoma, Clyde's gang shot

49
00:04:39,870 --> 00:04:46,620
a deputy named Eugene Moore, and now
they were wanted for killing a lawman.

50
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:52,090
Soon a sixteen-year-old
kid from the neighborhood,

51
00:04:52,110 --> 00:04:57,040
W.D. Jones, joined them,
mostly because it sounded exciting.

52
00:04:59,660 --> 00:05:03,687
Then in March 1933, Clyde's older
brother Buck was pardoned, and he

53
00:05:03,707 --> 00:05:09,000
and his wife Blanche came along for what
was supposed to be a short family visit.

54
00:05:12,140 --> 00:05:15,220
They rented an apartment above
a garage in Joplin, Missouri,

55
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:20,420
where the five of them tried very
hard to look like a normal family.

56
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:26,780
They played cards, drank and argued
so loudly that the neighbors called the

57
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:32,680
police, thinking they were bootleggers, so
who was really waiting behind that door?

58
00:05:35,300 --> 00:05:39,604
On April 13, 1933,
two officers walked up to the garage,

59
00:05:39,624 --> 00:05:44,040
and the gang answered
with gunfire from the windows.

60
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:50,870
Detective Harry McGinnis
and Constable Wes Harryman were killed,

61
00:05:50,890 --> 00:05:55,160
and the gang fled in such a hurry
that they left almost everything behind.

62
00:05:56,320 --> 00:05:57,650
Remember that camera?

63
00:05:57,670 --> 00:05:59,919
It was lying on the floor,
next to their guns,

64
00:05:59,939 --> 00:06:02,500
their clothes and one of Bonnie's poems.

65
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:06,330
The Joplin Globe developed the film,
and suddenly

66
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:11,960
a country deep in the Great Depression had
pictures of its new favorite villains.

67
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:18,790
People who had lost their jobs and farms
to the banks bought every edition,

68
00:06:18,810 --> 00:06:22,480
because these two looked like they
were having a much better time.

69
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:30,960
Bonnie loved the attention,
but she hated being called a cigar smoker,

70
00:06:30,980 --> 00:06:33,440
because she only ever smoked cigarettes.

71
00:06:35,520 --> 00:06:39,250
Two weeks later in Louisiana they stole
a car, and its owner,

72
00:06:39,270 --> 00:06:44,740
a young man named Dillard Darby,
chased them with a friend, Sophia Stone.

73
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:50,970
The gang caught them instead
and took them along, and when Bonnie

74
00:06:50,990 --> 00:06:56,120
asked Darby what he did for a living,
he said he was an undertaker.

75
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:03,020
Bonnie reportedly laughed and said
that one day he might have to work on her.

76
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:08,670
Later they let both hostages
go near Waldo, Arkansas,

77
00:07:08,690 --> 00:07:13,180
and even gave them five
dollars to get home.

78
00:07:16,340 --> 00:07:20,745
None of them knew yet that every
one of these clippings would end up

79
00:07:20,765 --> 00:07:25,720
in a single file, on the desk of the man
who would finally catch them.

80
00:07:27,840 --> 00:07:31,860
For now, their faces were
on wanted posters in five states,

81
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:36,400
but what happens when America's most
famous getaway driver misses a road sign?

82
00:07:40,040 --> 00:07:44,861
In June 1933, near Wellington, Texas,
Clyde was driving fast at night

83
00:07:44,881 --> 00:07:49,420
and missed the signs warning
that the bridge ahead was out.

84
00:07:55,260 --> 00:07:57,660
The car caught fire from the leaking gas,

85
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:02,760
and it scorched Bonnie's leg so badly
that she never walked properly again.

86
00:08:09,460 --> 00:08:12,650
A farm family took them
in and tried to help,

87
00:08:12,670 --> 00:08:16,060
but somebody called the sheriff,
and two lawmen arrived.

88
00:08:19,260 --> 00:08:24,600
The gang took both lawmen hostage,
drove them across the border into Oklahoma

89
00:08:24,620 --> 00:08:27,500
and left them tied
to a tree with barbed wire.

90
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:34,100
For weeks afterwards Clyde
carried Bonnie in his arms,

91
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:36,880
because she could no
longer stand on her own.

92
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:43,520
This was the glamorous outlaw life:
sleeping in stolen cars,

93
00:08:43,540 --> 00:08:47,000
eating cold beans from cans
and washing in rivers.

94
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:53,140
Bonnie still tried to look like
a movie star,

95
00:08:53,160 --> 00:08:56,260
even when her only mirror was
a cold river.

96
00:09:00,040 --> 00:09:04,789
Clyde preferred stolen Fords, and Henry
Ford even received a letter thanking

97
00:09:04,809 --> 00:09:10,040
him for such a fine getaway car, though
nobody is sure Clyde really wrote it.

98
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:16,725
In July 1933, police surrounded
their two motel cabins near Platte City,

99
00:09:16,745 --> 00:09:22,000
Missouri, and even brought an armored car;
the gang shot their way out,

100
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:26,380
but Buck was hit in the head, and flying
glass blinded Blanche in one eye.

101
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:36,230
Days later, in an Iowa park, a posse
of police and farmers found their camp,

102
00:09:36,250 --> 00:09:40,820
and Buck was captured, dying,
while Blanche refused to leave him.

103
00:09:44,460 --> 00:09:48,200
Clyde, Bonnie and W.D. waded across
a river and escaped,

104
00:09:48,220 --> 00:09:51,100
wounded, with almost nothing left.

105
00:09:54,220 --> 00:09:59,720
Soon even W.D. Jones slipped away
in the night and went home to his mother.

106
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:05,990
Bonnie and Clyde went home too,
in their own way:

107
00:10:06,010 --> 00:10:10,390
every few weeks they met their families
on dark country roads outside Dallas,

108
00:10:10,410 --> 00:10:13,320
bringing laundry
and taking home-cooked food.

109
00:10:16,960 --> 00:10:21,590
In November, the Dallas sheriff tried
to ambush one of these family picnics,

110
00:10:21,610 --> 00:10:26,600
and both of them escaped with bullets in
their legs, so why didn't they just stop?

111
00:10:30,260 --> 00:10:35,320
Instead, in January 1934, Clyde went
back to Eastham to free his friends,

112
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:38,300
while Bonnie honked the horn
in the fog to guide them.

113
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:48,060
Five prisoners got away, but a guard named
Joe Crowson was shot and later died.

114
00:10:50,140 --> 00:10:53,620
The head of the Texas prisons,
Lee Simmons,

115
00:10:53,640 --> 00:10:58,600
took it personally, and he went
looking for one particular man.

116
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:05,370
Frank Hamer was a retired Texas Ranger
who had survived dozens of gunfights,

117
00:11:05,390 --> 00:11:09,620
and in February 1934 he
quietly took the job.

118
00:11:11,740 --> 00:11:15,510
Police in five states had been chasing
Bonnie and Clyde for two years,

119
00:11:15,530 --> 00:11:20,480
and those chases usually ended the same
way, with the police car in a ditch.

120
00:11:23,220 --> 00:11:27,940
So Hamer did something nobody expected:
he stopped chasing them.

121
00:11:29,460 --> 00:11:33,690
Instead, he sat down with that file
of newspaper clippings and a map,

122
00:11:33,710 --> 00:11:36,460
and he waited for them
to make one mistake.

123
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:41,580
He underlined one line twice,
a line the papers reportedly

124
00:11:41,600 --> 00:11:45,560
quoted from Clyde after Eastham:
he would never go back,

125
00:11:45,580 --> 00:11:50,140
which meant there would never be
a surrender, only the question of where.

126
00:11:52,260 --> 00:11:55,050
On day one, Hamer started driving,
not after them,

127
00:11:55,070 --> 00:11:59,300
but through every town
where they had already been.

128
00:12:02,460 --> 00:12:06,600
Everywhere he went,
someone remembered them: a limping girl,

129
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:11,240
a young man in a hat,
a new Ford and a pile of empty cans.

130
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:19,928
By day twenty, he understood their main
trick: in those days, police could rarely

131
00:12:19,948 --> 00:12:26,060
follow a car across a state line,
so Clyde simply drove into the next state.

132
00:12:29,220 --> 00:12:31,534
So Hamer stopped marking
where they had been

133
00:12:31,554 --> 00:12:34,660
and started marking
where they kept coming back to.

134
00:12:37,660 --> 00:12:40,948
The loops always ran along state lines,
and sooner or later every one

135
00:12:40,968 --> 00:12:44,820
of them led home to their mothers,
so how long would Hamer have to wait?

136
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:54,785
On day fifty of Hamer's hunt, Easter
Sunday 1934, two young highway patrolmen

137
00:12:54,805 --> 00:13:00,540
stopped near Grapevine, Texas, to help
what they thought was a stranded car.

138
00:13:03,460 --> 00:13:07,000
They were shot before they
could draw their guns.

139
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:15,462
One of them, H.D. Murphy, was supposed
to marry his sweetheart, Maree Tullis,

140
00:13:15,482 --> 00:13:21,380
that month, and local historians say she
came to his funeral in her wedding dress.

141
00:13:23,560 --> 00:13:28,260
The papers claimed Bonnie had laughed over
a dying man, which was probably not true,

142
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:31,780
but after Grapevine nobody was
cheering for them anymore.

143
00:13:33,220 --> 00:13:36,780
Rewards were posted across the South,
and from then on every

144
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:40,900
farmer with a shotgun was watching
the road for a stolen Ford.

145
00:13:45,560 --> 00:13:48,910
Five days later, near Commerce, Oklahoma,

146
00:13:48,930 --> 00:13:53,600
their car slid into a ditch again,
and two lawmen walked over to help.

147
00:13:56,040 --> 00:13:59,810
Clyde killed Constable Cal Campbell
and took the police chief,

148
00:13:59,830 --> 00:14:02,100
Percy Boyd, as a hostage.

149
00:14:03,560 --> 00:14:08,260
They drove Boyd around for hours,
and before letting him go in Kansas,

150
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:11,180
Bonnie gave him one message
for the newspapers.

151
00:14:13,340 --> 00:14:16,650
Not that she was innocent,
and not that she was sorry,

152
00:14:16,670 --> 00:14:20,120
only that Bonnie Parker
did not smoke cigars.

153
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:26,740
The papers printed that too, and one of
them landed on Hamer's desk, next to a map

154
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:31,740
that was finally full, but he still
needed someone to tell him the day.

155
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:40,400
Hamer found that someone in Ivy Methvin,
the father of Henry Methvin, a young

156
00:14:40,420 --> 00:14:46,980
convict they had freed at Eastham: help
set a trap, and your son will be pardoned.

157
00:14:49,960 --> 00:14:53,340
Around that time, Bonnie gave her mother
a new poem, which said

158
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:58,020
that one day she and Clyde would go down
together and be buried side by side.

159
00:15:00,160 --> 00:15:04,200
Then they drove on, because
a car was the only home they had left.

160
00:15:06,360 --> 00:15:08,190
On the road outside Shreveport,

161
00:15:08,210 --> 00:15:12,940
they picked up one of the Eastham
prisoners, a boy named Henry Methvin.

162
00:15:14,360 --> 00:15:19,480
On May 21, Henry Methvin got separated
from Bonnie and Clyde in Shreveport,

163
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:23,590
and Hamer knew where he would go:
home to his father's farm,

164
00:15:23,610 --> 00:15:25,580
where they would come looking for him.

165
00:15:28,560 --> 00:15:33,254
On the night of May 22, Hamer and five
other lawmen hid in the bushes along

166
00:15:33,274 --> 00:15:37,151
a country road in Louisiana,
and they waited there until dawn,

167
00:15:37,171 --> 00:15:39,520
swatting mosquitoes in silence.

168
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:44,680
Hamer had brought his old
partner Manny Gault,

169
00:15:44,700 --> 00:15:49,480
two Dallas deputies who knew Clyde
by sight, Bob Alcorn and Ted Hinton,

170
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:54,720
and the local sheriff, Henderson Jordan,
with his deputy, Prentiss Oakley.

171
00:15:57,100 --> 00:16:01,150
On the morning of day one hundred and two,
May 23,

172
00:16:01,170 --> 00:16:05,360
Ivy Methvin's truck stood
by the road on a jack as bait.

173
00:16:08,260 --> 00:16:10,210
Clyde was driving in his socks,

174
00:16:10,230 --> 00:16:13,660
and Bonnie was eating
a sandwich from a café in town.

175
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:25,880
The lawmen fired more than
a hundred rounds in a few seconds,

176
00:16:25,900 --> 00:16:29,140
and it was over before Clyde
could reach for a gun.

177
00:16:31,700 --> 00:16:36,020
Within hours thousands of people crowded
into the little town of Arcadia,

178
00:16:36,040 --> 00:16:41,020
and souvenir hunters cut locks
of Bonnie's hair and pieces of her dress.

179
00:16:45,640 --> 00:16:47,860
And according to local accounts,

180
00:16:47,880 --> 00:16:52,140
the undertaker Bonnie had laughed
at a year earlier helped prepare her body.

181
00:16:54,200 --> 00:16:58,779
Her poem got almost everything right
except the last line: her mother refused

182
00:16:58,799 --> 00:17:03,440
to let them share a grave, so they
were buried in different cemeteries.

183
00:17:07,560 --> 00:17:10,370
Even their bullet-riddled
car went on tour,

184
00:17:10,390 --> 00:17:13,800
and people paid to look at it
at fairs across the country.

185
00:17:17,460 --> 00:17:21,980
Blanche served six years in prison
and lived quietly until 1988,

186
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,380
the only one of the four who grew old.

187
00:17:26,500 --> 00:17:31,340
Hamer rarely talked about that morning,
and he never wrote a book about it.

188
00:17:33,460 --> 00:17:38,020
Thirty years later, a Hollywood hit made
Bonnie and Clyde young and beautiful,

189
00:17:38,040 --> 00:17:41,130
and showed Hamer captured
and humiliated by the gang,

190
00:17:41,150 --> 00:17:44,800
something that never happened,
so his family sued the studio.

191
00:17:47,760 --> 00:17:53,840
And in 2026, a new video game built
its heroes on the same old romance.

192
00:17:56,860 --> 00:17:59,730
The real Bonnie
and Clyde were two broke kids from Dallas

193
00:17:59,750 --> 00:18:04,470
who left at least nine dead lawmen behind
them; newspapers made them legends,

194
00:18:04,490 --> 00:18:07,580
and a patient man reading
their file brought them down.

195
00:18:09,760 --> 00:18:15,180
Thank you for watching! If you like my
videos, support me, and I'll do my best.

